  "live_records": {
    "q-109657": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109657",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "If ${X}_{1}∼N(-1,{6}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(5,{8}^{2})$  are independent, then what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{2}-{X}_{1}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ then \n\n$$\n{X}_{2}-{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{2}-{\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{2}-{X}_{1} & ∼N(5-(-1),{6}^{2}+{8}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(6,100) \\\\  & ∼N(6,{10}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $Y∼N(6,{10}^{2}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
