    "q-109647": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109647",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "If ${X}_{1}∼N(7,16)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(-2,4)$ are independent, then what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ then \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2} & ∼N(7+(-2),16+4) \\\\  & ∼N(5,20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $Y∼N(5,20).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
