    "q-109650": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109650",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "If ${X}_{1}∼N(5,{4}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(-1,{1}^{2})$ are independent, then what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+3{X}_{2} & ∼N(1\\cdot 5+3\\cdot (-1),{1}^{2}\\cdot 16+{3}^{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & ∼N(2,{5}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y∼N(2,{5}^{2}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
